        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing a Mat to Your Car and Bay
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the car, not the garage. A mat needs to reach from just in front of the front bumper to just behind the rear tires, and wide enough that both sets of tires land on it with a few inches to spare on each side. For containment mats this matters more than for any other type, because the slush that falls off a car mostly comes from the wheel arches and the front bumper, and a mat that stops short catches the least of it. As a rough guide, compact cars suit mats around 14 to 16 feet long, mid-size cars and SUVs 16 to 18 feet, and full-size trucks 20 feet or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width is where most people go wrong in the other direction. A mat that fills the bay leaves nowhere to walk without stepping on it, and raised containment edges become a trip hazard alongside the car. Aim for a mat roughly a foot wider than the car's track and leave a clear walkway on the side you get out. Common single-bay widths sit between about 7 and 8.5 feet, which suits most cars and SUVs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the door. Mats should stop short of the garage door's closing line so the door's bottom seal lands on concrete, not on the mat edge. A door closing onto a mat lifts the seal and lets drafts and water in, and a containment mat's raised edge under the door will be crushed every time it closes. If your door seal is already worn, a new seal is a cheap fix; see our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-door-bottom-seal/">best garage door bottom seals</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the floor's slope. Most garage floors fall toward the door so water drains out. A ribbed vinyl pad works with that slope, and a containment mat works against it by holding water, so on a steep floor make sure the containment mat sits flat across the low end or the water will all collect at one edge and overtop it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="snowmelt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Snow, Salt and Containment Mats
          </h2>

          <p>
            A car can carry a surprising amount of snow into the garage. Packed slush in the wheel arches, on the bumpers and on the roof all melts overnight, and the water that runs off carries road salt and grit with it. On bare concrete that water spreads, finds the low spots and soaks in, and repeated salt exposure is one of the main reasons garage floors scale and pit. Our guide to <a href="/does-salt-damage-concrete/">whether salt damages concrete</a> explains the mechanism; the short version is that keeping salty meltwater off the slab is worth doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Containment mats solve the spread, not the water. The edges hold it, and you still have to remove it. In practice that means pushing it out the door with a squeegee every day or two during a cold snap, or picking it up with a wet-dry vac. A small shop vac makes the job quick; our guide to <a href="/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/">vacuuming water with a shop vac</a> covers the filter and float settings that keep the motor safe. Left alone, the water sits, refreezes on cold nights and leaves a crust of salt when it eventually evaporates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edge design is the main difference between containment mats. Some use a rigid lip, which holds water well but can crack in deep cold or crease where tires cross it. Others use foam or sponge columns, like the GaraMat, which compress as you drive over them and spring back. Either way, try to drive straight on and off rather than turning the wheel while a tire sits on the edge, which is how edges get torn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a containment mat clean between storms. A layer of salt and grit dried onto the surface will scratch the PVC and make it slippery when wet. A rinse with a hose on a warm day, followed by a squeegee, is enough. Do not use ice melt on the mat itself; it adds more salt to what you are trying to contain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Oil and Fluid Drips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil, coolant, brake fluid and transmission fluid are the other big threats to a garage floor, and they are harder to fix than water once they soak in. Oil penetrates porous concrete and leaves a stain that takes a poultice or a dedicated cleaner to lift; our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a> covers the methods, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> covers the products. Preventing the stain in the first place is far cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a single known drip, a small absorbent drip mat under the leak point is the most sensible answer. It costs little, catches what falls, and can be replaced when it is saturated. Position it under the engine or transmission rather than centered under the car, and check it every week or two so you notice if the leak is getting worse. A fresh puddle of red or green fluid is worth investigating promptly, since transmission fluid and coolant leaks can become expensive repairs if ignored.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an older car that drips a little of several fluids, a full-bay absorbent mat makes more sense, because the drips will not all land in one place. Choose one with a genuine waterproof backing; an absorbent mat without one just holds the oil against the concrete for longer. Accept that it will stain, and plan on cleaning or replacing it eventually.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coolant deserves a special mention because it is sweet-tasting and toxic to pets. Absorbent mats help by soaking up drips rather than leaving puddles, but if you have pets that use the garage, clean up coolant leaks promptly and keep the mat itself out of reach when it is contaminated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installing and Trimming a Mat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a clean, dry floor. Sweep and, if there is old oil, degrease it first; a mat laid over an oily patch can trap the oil and make it slide. Let the floor dry fully, especially before laying vinyl, because moisture trapped under a solid sheet can encourage mildew and keep the concrete damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unroll the mat and let it relax before you trim it. Vinyl and PVC mats are shipped rolled and will curl at the ends for a day or more, longer in a cold garage. Laying them out in a warm space, or on a sunny day with the door open, speeds this up. Trimming a curled mat leads to crooked cuts, so wait until it lies flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trim with a sharp utility knife against a straightedge, or with heavy scissors for fabric mats. Cut a little at a time, especially around posts or steps, and remember that most mats should stop short of the garage door's closing line. For vinyl pads, the listings for our top pick say they can go down loose or with adhesive; loose is easier to undo, while double-sided tape at the corners stops a light mat creeping when tires roll over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Containment mats need one extra step: make sure the raised edges are fully upright all the way round, and that the mat sits flat with no folds where water can escape. Drive on slowly and straight the first time to settle it into place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cleaning and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl parking pads are the easiest to maintain. Sweep or blow them off, and mop with mild detergent and water when they get grimy. Avoid harsh solvents, which can soften or discolor PVC. Most owners find tire marks lift with an all-purpose cleaner and a stiff brush.
          </p>

          <p>
            Containment mats need emptying more than cleaning. During winter, squeegee or vacuum out the water regularly. In spring, take the mat outside, rinse off the dried salt and grit, let it dry, and either put it back or roll it loosely for storage. Do not fold a PVC mat tightly in the cold, which can crease or crack it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Absorbent fabric mats vary. The Armor All listing at Lowe's says to scrub with soap, hose off and hang to dry, and to avoid bleach. Other absorbent mats do not publish care instructions, so check before you pressure-wash one, since high pressure can tear fabric tops and force water through seams. Expect oil to leave a mark no matter how you clean it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rubber rolls clean with a broom and a damp mop, and household cleaners. New rubber often smells strongly for the first few weeks; airing the garage and leaving the roll unrolled outdoors for a day or two before installation helps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Garage Floor Mats Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small drip mats are the cheapest option, typically priced well under the cost of a tank of fuel, and they are the right purchase if you only need to catch one leak. Full-bay absorbent mats and budget containment mats sit in the next tier, and premium containment mats and thick vinyl parking pads cost more again. Retail prices for single-bay vinyl pads we saw listed reached several hundred dollars, while absorbent mats in similar sizes were often listed for less. Prices move often, so treat these as rough tiers rather than quotes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare the cost per year rather than the sticker price. A containment mat that survives five winters is cheaper than a budget mat replaced every season, and a vinyl pad that lasts the life of the house costs little per year. Absorbent mats are consumables: they work well until they are saturated or stained, then they are replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that against a coating. A good epoxy or polyaspartic floor costs far more upfront than any mat, but protects the whole floor permanently against oil and salt. Our guides to the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> and the <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">best garage floor coatings</a> cover those options. Many owners end up with both: a coated floor, with a containment mat in winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are protecting a rental or a garage you plan to leave in a few years, mats win easily. You can take them with you, and they leave the floor as you found it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When to Coat the Floor Instead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mats are a fix for one parking bay. If the whole garage floor is stained, cracked or dusting, a mat hides part of the problem but does nothing for the rest. In that case, repairing and coating the floor gives you a surface that resists oil, salt and tire marks everywhere, and makes cleanup a matter of a mop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coatings also make more sense if you work in the garage, roll a floor jack around, or use it as a gym or workshop. Thin PVC mats do not like jacks, stands or dragged tools, and absorbent mats are awkward to work on. A coated floor, with a rubber mat only where you stand at the bench, is a more practical setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip a mat, too, if the floor has an active moisture problem. A solid vinyl or rubber sheet laid on a damp slab can trap moisture and encourage mildew underneath. Fix the moisture first. The same rule applies before coating: our guide to <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/">testing a garage floor for moisture</a> explains the simple plastic-sheet test.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, a mat is the fastest, cheapest and most reversible way to protect the floor. Choose the type for your mess, size it to your car, and keep it clean, and it will save the concrete underneath for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Containment Mat for Oil Drips</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Containment mats hold water but do not absorb anything. An oil drip on a PVC containment mat just sits there, gets tracked around and can make the surface slippery. For drips, use an absorbent mat with a waterproof backing, or a small drip pad under the leak.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a Mat That Is Too Short</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most slush falls from the front bumper and the wheel arches. A mat that stops at the front tires catches the least of it. Measure from just ahead of the front bumper to just behind the rear tires, and choose the next size up if you are close.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Garage Door Close on the Mat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A door closing on the mat lifts its seal, lets in drafts and water, and crushes containment edges. Trim the mat so it stops short of the door's closing line and the seal lands on bare concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Emptying a Containment Mat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A containment mat only moves the problem from the floor to the mat. Left full, the water refreezes and leaves salt behind as it evaporates. Squeegee or vacuum it out every day or two during snowy spells.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying Vinyl on a Damp or Oily Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture trapped under a solid sheet keeps the slab damp and can encourage mildew, and oil underneath makes the mat slide. Clean, degrease and dry the floor first, and fix any moisture problem before laying a solid mat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best garage floor mat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the mess. For general protection of a single parking bay, a vinyl roll-out pad such as the G-Floor Ribbed is the most versatile choice. For snow and salt, choose a containment mat with raised edges, such as the GaraDry GaraMat. For oil and fluid drips, choose an absorbent mat with a waterproof backing, or a small drip pad under a single leak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do garage containment mats really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for snowmelt. Their raised edges hold meltwater, salt and grit so it does not spread across the floor. They do not get rid of the water, though; you still need to squeegee it out or pick it up with a wet-dry vac every day or two during snowy weather, or it will sit, refreeze and leave salt behind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a garage floor mat stop oil stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An absorbent mat with a genuine waterproof backing will catch drips before they reach the concrete, which prevents new stains. The mat itself will stain, though. A containment or vinyl mat will keep oil off the concrete too, but leaves it sitting on the surface, where it can be tracked around and make the mat slippery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a mat on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Many owners use a containment mat on a coated floor in winter to keep salty meltwater off and stop it spreading. Make sure the floor and the mat are clean and dry, and avoid rubber directly on the coating under parked tires, since some rubber compounds can mark or stain coatings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should a garage floor mat be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For vinyl parking pads, listings commonly quote about 50 to 75 mil at the base, which is thick enough to resist tire scuffing. Containment mats are often thinner because they rely on their edges rather than thickness. For a workbench or walkway, 3 mm or thicker rubber is more comfortable and durable. Thickness matters less than choosing the right type.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to glue down a garage floor mat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most containment, absorbent and vinyl mats are designed to lie loose, and heavy vinyl pads stay put under their own weight. A few strips of double-sided tape at the corners stop lighter mats creeping. Adhesive makes a vinyl pad more permanent, but also harder to remove later.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean a garage floor mat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl pads clean with a broom and a mop with mild detergent. Containment mats need emptying in winter and a rinse in spring. Some absorbent mats, such as the Armor All mat sold at Lowe's, are listed as washable with soap and a hose; check the care instructions before pressure-washing any fabric mat.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Garage Floor Guides</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
